    "body": "## Background\n\nFollow-up to #44273. The existing PR fixed `torch.nonzero() → nonzero(as_tuple=True)` in several locations but missed these GPU attention backend hot paths.\n\n## Problem\n\n`torch.nonzero()` with default `as_tuple=False` forces an implicit CPU-GPU synchronization because the output tensor size is unknown and requires dynamic allocation. Forcing this sync in attention backend hot paths adds avoidable latency.\n\n`nonzero(as_tuple=True)` returns a tuple of 1D tensors with **zero** synchronization overhead.\n\n## Solution\n\nReplaced 3 GPU hot-path call sites that used `.squeeze(1)` or `.flatten()` on the result — both are equivalent to `[0]` indexing into the tuple form:\n\n| File | Before | After |\n|------|--------|-------|\n| `dsv4/v41_indexer/scoring.py:300` | `.nonzero().squeeze(1)` | `.nonzero(as_tuple=True)[0]` |\n| `deepseek_v4_backend.py:1816` | `.nonzero().squeeze(1)` | `.nonzero(as_tuple=True)[0]` |\n| `qwen_sparse_attn_backend.py:1225` | `.nonzero().flatten()` | `.nonzero(as_tuple=True)[0]` |\n\nThese are in the **GPU attention backend hot paths** (DSv4 indexer, DeepSeek V4 backend, Qwen sparse attention), where every forward call goes through them.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- Each eliminated sync saves 10–100µs per call (tensor-size dependent)\n- Compounds across every decode step in high-concurrency scenarios\n- Zero behavior change — `as_tuple=True` + `[0]` returns the same 1D tensor\n\n## Testing\n- Python syntax verified on all 3 files\n- No API contract change\n- Follows existing precedent in PR #44273\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:x: [Run #38563549022](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/38563549022)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #38563548955](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/38563548955)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\nLatest PR Test (AMD ROCm 10): <!-- 